---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { APP_STORE_AVAILABLE } from '../consts';

const sections = [
  { id: 'quick-start', label: 'Quick start' },
  { id: 'commands', label: 'Commands' },
  { id: 'options', label: 'Options' },
  { id: 'runtime', label: 'Optimization behavior' },
  { id: 'examples', label: 'Useful examples' },
  { id: 'output', label: 'JSON output and results' },
  { id: 'progress', label: 'Progress events' },
  { id: 'measurements', label: 'Savings and measurements' },
  { id: 'settings-activity', label: 'Settings and Activity' },
  { id: 'exit-codes', label: 'Exit codes and cancellation' },
  { id: 'agents', label: 'AI agent workflow' },
  { id: 'permissions', label: 'Permissions and safety' },
];

const commands = [
  ['paths', 'List saved read/write authorizations and whether they are available.'],
  ['locations', 'Read saved locations, linked apps, last known status, size, and measured savings.'],
  ['savings', 'Read current compression and deduplication savings, plus recent savings.'],
  ['overview', 'Read the Overview information, including home-volume capacity and monitoring status.'],
  ['settings', 'Inspect optimization settings, performance profiles, and exclusion rules.'],
  ['activity', 'Read retained per-file results, newest first, with cursor-based pagination.'],
  ['scan', 'Inspect the selected targets and explain eligibility without optimizing files.'],
  ['optimize', 'Scan targets and combine file compression with file deduplication using DiskPress settings. Deduplication must be enabled.'],
];

const options = [
  ['--json', 'Emit newline-delimited JSON events on standard output.'],
  ['--quiet', 'Suppress intermediate progress while keeping the final result.'],
  ['--gui', 'Immediately show a progress window with Cancel for scan or optimize. It closes when the command finishes and does not grant permissions or ask for confirmation.'],
  ['--no-gui', 'Run without opening a window. This is the default. Do not combine it with --gui.'],
  ['--ignore-exclusions', 'For scan or optimize, override configured exclusions for explicitly named files only. Folder contents still follow exclusions. Safety checks and compression size limits still apply.'],
  ['--limit N', 'Activity page size from 1 to 500. The default is 50. Only for activity.'],
  ['--before ID', 'Continue Activity using the nextBefore cursor from the preceding result. Only for activity.'],
  ['--', 'End options before paths, including filenames beginning with a dash.'],
  ['-h, --help', 'Print the built-in help without starting the app service.'],
  ['--version', 'Print the installed version.'],
];

const progressFields = [
  ['phase', 'The current stage, such as finding_duplicates, grouping_duplicates, comparing, compressing, verifying, or deduplicating. Stages can change without an active file.'],
  ['phaseProcessedFiles / phaseTotalFiles', 'Optimization progress for the current stage. These counts can restart between stages. A zero phaseTotalFiles means the total is unknown, so show indeterminate progress.'],
  ['processedFiles / totalFiles', 'Job-wide counts. During discovering, processedFiles counts files found so far and totalFiles is zero. Do not treat discovery as completed optimization.'],
  ['currentPath / currentFile', 'currentPath identifies a scan item. currentFile is an optimization display name, not necessarily a full path. An absent or empty value clears the previous item from your display.'],
];

const exitCodes = [
  ['0', 'Completed. This can include safe skips or zero savings.'],
  ['1', 'The operation failed or recovery needs attention.'],
  ['2', 'Partial success. Some files could not be optimized or read, or a statistics section failed.'],
  ['64', 'Invalid command or arguments.'],
  ['75', 'The optimization service is unavailable, or a final result could not be delivered. Review Activity and any recovery message before retrying.'],
  ['77', 'A target lacks a usable saved authorization.'],
  ['130', 'Cancelled. Wait for this exit before treating the operation as stopped.'],
];
---

<DocumentLayout title="Command-line guide" eyebrow="DiskPress CLI" heading="Your automation. The same native engine." description="Use DiskPress from Terminal, scripts, or AI agents. Inspect first, optimize selected files, and get structured results without opening the main window." sections={sections}>
  {!APP_STORE_AVAILABLE && <div class="notice"><p><strong>DiskPress will be available soon.</strong> These instructions apply once the app is installed. The CLI is included with the app, not a separate download. See <a href="/faq/#download-price">release availability</a>.</p></div>}

  <section id="quick-start">
    <h2>Start with the installed app</h2>
    <p>The CLI is the executable inside the app bundle. There is no separate command-line download. These examples use the Mac App Store copy at <code>/Applications/DiskPress.app</code>. Adjust the executable path if your copy is elsewhere.</p>
    <CodeBlock id="cli-help" label="Read the current command reference" code={'"/Applications/DiskPress.app/Contents/MacOS/DiskPress" --help'} />
    <p>For Setapp, use the executable inside your Setapp installation. Its bundle name and location may differ. The Mac App Store and Setapp editions keep separate settings and saved authorizations, so configure the same edition you invoke. Its built-in <code>--help</code> shows examples using that installation's executable path.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open that copy of DiskPress and go to Settings, then Authorized Paths.</li>
      <li>Add the specific files or folders the command should be allowed to read and write. For a compressed file, authorize its containing folder.</li>
      <li>Inspect the selection with <code>scan</code> before choosing <code>optimize</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="notice"><p>A CLI-only launch starts the DiskPress process without recurring monitoring, automatic startup registration, or an initial saved-location savings scan. After optimization, it refreshes saved-location savings in the background. The CLI result can arrive first, and the host remains open until its commands and that read-only refresh finish. An app that was already open stays open.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="commands">
    <h2>Commands</h2>
    <p>Run these after the executable path. Only <code>scan</code> and <code>optimize</code> accept target paths, with a maximum of 500 per request.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="CLI commands" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Command</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>{commands.map(([command, description]) => <tr><td><code>{command}</code></td><td>{description}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p><code>paths</code> means permissions. <code>locations</code> means saved optimization targets. They are not interchangeable. Configuration changes and new permission grants remain in the app's Settings.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="options">
    <h2>Options</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="CLI options" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>{options.map(([option, description]) => <tr><td><code>{option}</code></td><td>{description}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p><code>--json</code> and <code>--quiet</code> work with all eight commands above. Report commands take no target paths and do not accept <code>--gui</code> or <code>--ignore-exclusions</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="runtime">
    <h2>What to expect during optimization</h2>
    <h3>The first duplicate check can take longer</h3>
    <p>With deduplication enabled, DiskPress combines both techniques in one run. The first duplicate check can take a long time for large folders, including when files are already compressed. Later runs reuse cached fingerprints for unchanged files, even after restarting DiskPress. Matches still undergo content and metadata verification before replacement.</p>
    <p>DiskPress also reuses scan results within an optimization and can remember unchanged files that did not benefit from compression. Avoiding another compression attempt does not remove those files from deduplication checks.</p>
    <p>For compression-only runs, turn off "Deduplicate identical files" in Settings. There is no per-command compression-only flag. CLI runs do not show the first-use deduplication confirmation, including with <code>--gui</code>.</p>
    <h3>Recent File Protection does not delay CLI requests</h3>
    <p>Recent File Protection delays recently changed files during saved-location optimization, including automatic monitoring and Optimize Now. CLI <code>scan</code> and <code>optimize</code> are explicit one-time requests and do not apply this delay, with or without <code>--gui</code>. This is also true when the selected target is a saved location.</p>
    <p>The <code>recentFileProtectionSeconds</code> setting reports the saved-location delay, not a CLI waiting period. Schedule commands after the program producing your files has finished writing. Active-writer detection, file-change checks, and verification still apply.</p>
    <h3>Linked apps still protect their locations</h3>
    <p>If a saved location has a Linked App, optimization skips its files while that app is running. This also applies to files you name individually through the CLI, and <code>--ignore-exclusions</code> does not bypass it. Quit the linked app before optimizing those files.</p>
    <p><code>locations --json</code> includes <code>linkedApplication</code> with <code>bundleIdentifier</code>, <code>path</code>, and <code>name</code>, or <code>null</code> when none is configured. This describes the saved association, not a live running-app status. A preliminary <code>scan</code> is not a guarantee that a file will pass every check during optimization. See the <a href="/faq/#linked-app">Linked App FAQ</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Useful examples</h2>
    <p>For convenience in one Terminal session, store the executable path in a variable. This does not install a command or change your shell configuration.</p>
    <CodeBlock id="cli-variable" code={'DISKPRESS="/Applications/DiskPress.app/Contents/MacOS/DiskPress"'} />
    <h3>Inspect your permissions and settings</h3>
    <CodeBlock id="cli-inspect" code={'"$DISKPRESS" --version\n"$DISKPRESS" paths --json\n"$DISKPRESS" settings --json\n"$DISKPRESS" locations --json'} />
    <h3>Scan without optimizing</h3>
    <p>These examples use a folder of completed data. Replace the path with a specific, mostly stable folder you intend to optimize, and authorize it first. A scan does not predict the final compression ratio, and some compatibility checks only happen during real optimization.</p>
    <CodeBlock id="cli-scan" code={'"$DISKPRESS" scan --json -- "$HOME/Documents/Completed Data"'} />
    <h3>Run a one-time optimization</h3>
    <p>This changes the storage of supported files in the selected folder and can add disk writes. Avoid repeatedly optimizing locations with frequent new or changing files. See the <a href="/faq/#ssd-wear">SSD-write guidance</a> before scheduling commands.</p>
    <CodeBlock id="cli-optimize" code={'"$DISKPRESS" optimize --json --quiet -- "$HOME/Documents/Completed Data"'} />
    <h3>Show progress when you want it</h3>
    <CodeBlock id="cli-gui" code={'"$DISKPRESS" optimize --gui -- "$HOME/Documents/Completed Data"'} />
    <h3>Read the results</h3>
    <CodeBlock id="cli-results" code={'"$DISKPRESS" overview --json\n"$DISKPRESS" savings --json\n"$DISKPRESS" activity --limit 20 --json'} />
    <p>The command result describes the finished job. Saved-location totals may still be refreshing. Check <code>savings.refreshing</code> and the measurement dates before treating those totals as updated.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="output">
    <h2>Understand the output</h2>
    <p>Human-readable progress goes to standard error, with the final summary on standard output. With <code>--json</code>, standard output is a stream of separate JSON objects, one per line. Parse it as NDJSON, not one large JSON document.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Events include <code>schemaVersion</code>, <code>requestID</code>, <code>sequence</code>, and <code>event</code>. The current schema version is 1.</li>
      <li>Event types are <code>progress</code>, <code>result</code>, and <code>error</code>. Progress is sampled, so sequence numbers can have gaps.</li>
      <li>The final event includes <code>exitCode</code> and <code>message</code>. A <code>result</code> event can describe partial success or cancellation, so check the exit code too.</li>
      <li>CLI labels, help, and generated messages use English. Saved records and shared diagnostics retain their original text. The optional progress window follows the app language.</li>
      <li>Use JSON field names, structured reason codes where provided, and exit codes for automation. Do not match translated or human-readable message text.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Scan and optimization results</h3>
    <p><code>scan</code> returns <code>totalFiles</code>, <code>eligibleFiles</code>, <code>potentialDeduplicationFiles</code>, <code>logicalBytes</code>, <code>allocatedBytes</code>, <code>alreadySavedBytes</code>, and the applied <code>minimumFileBytes</code> and <code>maximumFileBytes</code>. Potential deduplication candidates are not confirmed duplicates or a savings prediction.</p>
    <p>Scan <code>reasons</code> contain <code>code</code>, <code>count</code>, and <code>message</code>. Reasons can overlap, and an excluded folder counts once because its contents are not enumerated.</p>
    <p><code>optimize</code> returns <code>targets</code>, <code>totalFiles</code>, <code>processedFiles</code>, <code>compressedFiles</code>, <code>deduplicatedFiles</code>, <code>skippedFiles</code>, and <code>failedFiles</code>, plus its initial <code>scan</code> summary. Its top-level <code>reasons</code> are recent human-readable skip or error summaries, not the full per-file history. Read <code>activity</code> for individual outcomes.</p>
    <p>Additional outcome counters include <code>alreadyCompressedFiles</code>, <code>insufficientSavingsFiles</code>, and <code>noBenefitFiles</code>. These explain outcomes, not extra files to add to the job total.</p>
    <p>Savings appear as <code>compressionSavedBytes</code>, <code>estimatedDeduplicationSavedBytes</code>, and <code>totalEstimatedSavedBytes</code>. Numeric sizes are bytes. Compression and deduplication can affect the same file, so do not add those two file counts to calculate distinct optimized files. Deduplication savings are estimates of shared storage, not a guarantee of an equal increase in free space. Zero savings can be a successful result.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="progress">
    <h2>Read progress as snapshots</h2>
    <p>Each progress event describes the latest state, not an increment to add to the previous event. Use stage counters for a progress bar and job-wide counters for the overall job.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="CLI progress fields" tabindex="0"><table class="cli-progress-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">JSON field</th><th scope="col">How to use it</th></tr></thead><tbody>{progressFields.map(([field, description]) => <tr><td><code>{field}</code></td><td>{description}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p class="fine">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens to read the full reference.</p>
    <p>Finding and grouping duplicates can take time without an active filename. Clear old file labels when the new snapshot has no current item. Do not assume a fixed stage order or that a stage reaching its total means the entire command has finished. Only the final event and process exit establish completion.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="measurements">
    <h2>Measurements are not all live scans</h2>
    <p><code>locations</code>, <code>savings</code>, and <code>overview</code> read saved measurements without scanning target files, requesting new permissions, or starting monitoring. They include <code>readAt</code>, <code>dataSource</code>, <code>locationCount</code>, <code>enabledLocationCount</code>, and <code>savings</code>. Dates are ISO 8601 in UTC.</p>
    <h3>Missing and outdated are different</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>measurementAvailable</code> indicates that a location has a saved measurement. Unknown location values are <code>null</code>, not zero.</li>
      <li><code>measurementStale</code> marks a retained measurement after a failed refresh. DiskPress keeps the last successful values instead of replacing them with zero. Read <code>measurementError</code> and <code>measuredAt</code> for context.</li>
      <li><code>savings.complete</code> is false if any location is unmeasured or has a stale measurement. <code>unmeasuredLocationCount</code>, <code>staleLocationCount</code>, and <code>oldestMeasurementAt</code> describe coverage and age.</li>
      <li>A saved location's <code>statusSource</code> is <code>last_saved</code>. Its status is not a fresh scan of the target.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Current savings cover measured saved locations, including disabled ones, with overlapping files counted once. A location can have a nonzero size but no credited savings because shared files are already counted elsewhere. Recent savings describe completed optimization work. Do not add Activity records to current totals or assume every record is a unique file.</p>
    <p>On Catalina, current deduplication savings cannot be measured. <code>deduplicationMeasurementAvailable</code> is false and those values are <code>null</code>. Deduplication can still run there, and recent savings can include its completed work.</p>
    <h3>The refresh after optimization</h3>
    <p>After an optimization run, DiskPress refreshes saved-location savings in the background. The CLI can return its final result before this read-only refresh finishes. Reports expose <code>savings.refreshing</code> and <code>savings.refreshError</code>. Use the measurement dates and coverage flags as well, since a finished refresh is not proof that every location was measured successfully.</p>
    <p>The host remains open until its commands and the refresh finish, without enabling recurring monitoring. A selection <code>scan</code> or report command alone does not request this refresh. Use Rescan in the app's Overview when you want to refresh saved measurements separately.</p>
    <h3>Storage and monitoring</h3>
    <p><code>savings</code> and <code>overview</code> also include <code>recentSavings</code> for the last 24 hours, 7 days, and 30 days. <code>overview</code> adds <code>storage</code> for the home volume and <code>monitoring</code>. Disk capacity is queried at request time, independently of saved file measurements.</p>
    <p><code>monitoring.enabled</code> is a saved preference. It does not mean the CLI started monitoring. Check <code>monitoring.serviceRunning</code> and <code>monitoring.liveStatusAvailable</code> before displaying live status. When live status is unavailable, live progress fields are <code>null</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="settings-activity">
    <h2>Inspect settings and Activity</h2>
    <p><code>settings --json</code> returns a <code>settings</code> object with <code>deduplicationEnabled</code>, <code>scanHiddenSystemFiles</code>, <code>recentFileProtectionSeconds</code>, <code>monitoringEnabled</code>, and <code>scanIntervalSeconds</code>. Reading these values does not change them.</p>
    <p>Compression thresholds are reported as <code>minimumFileBytes</code>, <code>maximumFileBytes</code>, <code>minimumCompressionSavingsFraction</code>, and <code>minimumCompressionSavingsBytes</code>. These settings describe compression eligibility, not a guarantee that every file will save space.</p>
    <p><code>settings.performance</code> includes <code>level</code>, <code>batteryLevel</code>, <code>separatePowerSettings</code>, and <code>pauseMonitoringOnBattery</code>. Profile values are <code>low_cpu</code>, <code>balanced</code>, <code>fast</code>, or <code>fastest</code>. The current power source and macOS can affect the active performance level.</p>
    <p><code>settings.exclusions</code> contains typed rules with <code>id</code>, <code>enabled</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>target</code>, <code>pattern</code>, <code>description</code>, and <code>extensions</code>. Types are <code>file_type</code>, <code>wildcard</code>, <code>regular_expression</code>, and <code>file_group</code>. A rule targets <code>file_name</code> or <code>full_path</code>. File groups include their extensions.</p>
    <h3>Page through retained results</h3>
    <p><code>activity --json</code> returns <code>activity.records</code> from both GUI and CLI work, newest first. Each record includes <code>id</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>path</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>outcome</code>, <code>originalAllocatedBytes</code>, <code>resultAllocatedBytes</code>, <code>savedBytes</code>, <code>durationSeconds</code>, <code>detail</code>, and <code>locationID</code>. Outcomes are <code>compressed</code>, <code>deduplicated</code>, <code>skipped</code>, <code>failed</code>, or <code>recovered</code>.</p>
    <p>To continue, pass the exact returned <code>activity.nextBefore</code> value to <code>--before</code> until <code>activity.hasMore</code> is false. The default page size is 50, and <code>--limit</code> accepts 1 through 500. Large records may shorten a page, so follow the cursor rather than the record count. New records do not shift existing cursors.</p>
    <p>A cleared or expired cursor returns an error. Restart without <code>--before</code>. Activity is retained history, not an exhaustive file inventory. Multiple records can refer to one file, and a missing record does not prove that a file was never optimized.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="exit-codes">
    <h2>Exit codes and safe cancellation</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="CLI exit codes" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Code</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>{exitCodes.map(([code, description]) => <tr><td><code>{code}</code></td><td>{description}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>Ctrl+C, SIGTERM, Cancel, or closing the progress window requests a safe stop. Let the process finish. Files already optimized remain optimized. Losing the terminal client or breaking its output pipe also cancels work.</p>
    <p>If standard output cannot accept a final result, the process exit code remains authoritative. Do not deliberately cut off a running optimization's output with a command such as <code>head</code>. Use <code>--quiet</code> when you only need the final result.</p>
    <p>Accepted requests are not replayed after a host crash. Exit 75 does not prove that no files changed. If the result could not be delivered, review Activity and any recovery message before deciding whether to retry.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="agents">
    <h2>A sensible AI agent workflow</h2>
    <p>DiskPress provides the commands. Your agent decides how to invoke them, so give it clear boundaries. A useful starting instruction is below.</p>
    <CodeBlock id="agent-instructions" label="Instructions for your agent" code={'Read --version and --help from my installed DiskPress executable.\nUse that same edition for permissions, settings, and commands.\nInspect paths, settings, and locations with --json.\nScan only the files or folders I explicitly select.\nRemember that CLI requests do not apply Recent File Protection.\nExplain eligibility and ask before optimizing.\nRespect linked apps and wait until files are no longer being written.\nDo not grant permissions or use elevated privileges.\nDo not override exclusions unless I explicitly request it.\nUse the final JSON event and exit code to report the job result.\nCheck refreshing, stale flags, and dates before reporting saved totals.\nOn a missing final result or recovery error, review Activity before retrying.'} />
    <p>Pass each path as a separate argument after <code>--</code>. Never concatenate an unescaped filename into a shell command. If permission is missing, ask the user to authorize the path in DiskPress instead of retrying in a loop or using <code>sudo</code>.</p>
    <p>Read Activity for per-file reasons after warnings or failures. A successful run can save nothing, and a safely skipped file is not necessarily an error. On a recovery error, preserve the files and report the message. Wait for process exit after requesting cancellation.</p>
    <div class="notice"><p>DiskPress does not call an AI service. An agent can receive private paths, filenames, and activity details through local output and may send them to its provider. The agent's settings and privacy policy determine that behavior.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="permissions">
    <h2>Permissions and safety still apply</h2>
    <p>All requested targets must be covered by usable saved read/write authorizations before optimization begins. If any target lacks access, the command fails before optimizing the other targets. The CLI never opens a permission picker, grants access, or bypasses protected paths. A selected folder can cover descendants, but authorizing one file does not authorize its siblings.</p>
    <p><code>paths --json</code> returns <code>authorizations</code> with <code>path</code>, <code>directory</code>, <code>access</code>, and <code>available</code>. The access value is <code>readWrite</code>. An unavailable grant may need the volume reconnected or the path authorized again in the same edition of DiskPress.</p>
    <p>The same compression and deduplication engine, verification, recovery, configured exclusions, and applicable file limits are used by the app and CLI. Apps and executables remain unsupported. Deduplication groups files only within the same compatible filesystem, never across volumes. An explicit exclusion override never disables safety checks or linked-app protection.</p>
    <p>Terminal scan and optimization jobs run one at a time in the current macOS login session and share the app's CPU scheduling, recovery, and accounting. Read-only statistics, settings, and Activity commands can run while work is in progress. There is no CLI command to enable a daemon, mutate settings, or undo completed storage changes.</p>
    <p>Keep an independent backup. See the <a href="/faq/#verification-backup">FAQ on verification and recovery</a> before automating important data.</p>
  </section>
</DocumentLayout>
